A||||7||RAREW66||Fiero Factory Subwoofer||||1||
Z||000000||RAREW66||06-12-2001||12:23 AM||||I need some information.<P>Went roaming the junk yards today and found a couple of 87 Trans Ams with the factory sub system. Short on time, I did not have time to locate the amp. Does anyone know where the amp is located in a 87 Trans Am? I did not see it under the dash or next to the center consule.<P>These cars have two sub speakers each, so for $3 ea. I have four sub speakers that test and sound great. It appears that the Firebird sub speakers have a larger magnet than the Fiero.<P>Any help appreciated.<P>Fred Bartemeyer<BR>Co-Chairman Heartland Fieros||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||06-12-2001||12:52 AM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||stsmithgt||06-12-2001||10:25 AM||||My suggestion would be to hit up a Trans Am forum for that info.  I am assuming of course that there's one out there as informative as this one is about Fieros. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||286SE'S||06-14-2001||04:29 PM||||The subwoofer amp in a firebird is vertically mounted  behind the passenger side of the dash. Imagine if there was a glove box it would be more to the left side of that area. it can be removed from below the dash after removing a lot of trim. It is identical to the Fiero Amp. Same size and connector.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||06-14-2001||08:33 PM||||The Firebird subs won't fit in the Fiero enclosure. On the left is a stock size Fiero sub, on the right is a Firebird sub.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/subsize.jpg"> <BR>I bought a whole case of new replacement Firebird subs (Pioneer PS5) before I realized they were bigger.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RAREW66||06-17-2001||05:07 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR>[B]The Firebird subs won't fit in the Fiero enclosure. On the left is a stock size Fiero sub, on the right is a Firebird sub.<P>The stock subwoofer speakers used in the 86-89 Firebirds are identical in the Fiero speakers. The frame is identcal; with the only difference being that the magnet is larger in size.<P>I installed one of the these Firebird speakers in to my 87 GT with no modification; simply R+R.<P>The sound of the speaker has a tighter, cleaner sound with a lot less distortion at high bass levels compared to my low mileage 86 GT.<P>I already checked the GM part #'s for the Firebird and found that the Firebird speaker and the Fiero speaker where discontined at the same time.<P>Thanks 286SE'S for the amp location. I went back yesterday and got both amps. It also happens that the harness from the radio to the amp is the same. So I spent $10 more dollars and have two amps, one that is not working but repairable.<P>Fred Bartemeyer<BR>Co-Chairman Heartland Fieros||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||06-17-2001||07:02 PM||||OK,<BR> The replacement firebird subs I got don't even come close to fitting into the Fiero enclosure. Maybe they are for a differnt year or something? I can't find the OEM part # that they replace anymore. I knew I had read somewhere that the firebird drivers were the same, that's why I bought a whole case. I was dissapointed when I got them and found out they were too big.||||reg||7
Z||000007||terryk||06-18-2001||01:49 AM||||I've used 5.25" drivers in every Fiero sub I've fixed. The more compliant the better.||||reg||1
